luajit_generators/s_expression_run_time/README.md:ChainTree S-Expression Engine (v2.5)A high-performance, two-tier behavior engine for embedded systems. This system combines the structural rigidity of flow-based programming (Node-RED style) with the dynamic expressiveness of Lisp-style S-Expressions.1. System Architecture: The "Two-Tier" ModelThe engine resolves the "Rigidity vs. Control" trade-off common in embedded firmware by splitting logic into two distinct tiers.Tier 1: The Macro Path (Structural / Node-RED Style)Paradigm: Flow-based / Topological.Nodes: pipeline, if, cond, dispatch.Behavior: Linear execution. The engine follows static links (first_child -> next_sibling).Constraint: Strictly governed by Return Codes (CFL_CONTINUE, CFL_HALT). Any return code other than continue stops the flow. This provides safety for high-level state machines.Tier 2: The Micro Path (Functional / S-Expression)Paradigm: Functional / Recursive.Mechanism: main nodes with PARAM_OPEN_CALL parameters.Behavior: Recursive evaluation inside a single node.Capabilities: Fills the gap in Tier 1 by enabling Decorators, Higher-Order Functions, and Complex Sequencing (e.g., Retry logic, Mathematical evaluation) without bloating the visual tree structure.2. Function Types & Execution ModelsThe DSL distinguishes between three types of functions, each with a specific lifecycle and persistence model tailored for embedded control.1. Oneshot Functions (@)Symbol: oneshot("NAME")Lifecycle: Fire-and-Forget.Use: Atomic side effects that must happen exactly once per activation cycle. Examples: LOG, LED_ON, RESET_COUNTER, SEND_EVENT.Action:The engine checks if the node has already fired (S_EXPR_NODE_FLAG_ONESHOT_FIRED).If No: It executes the C function immediately and sets the FIRED flag.If Yes: It skips execution instantly.Note: The flag is only cleared when the parent node resets (e.g., a pipeline restarting).2. Boolean Functions (?)Symbol: bool_fn("NAME") or pred_ref("NAME")Lifecycle: Quasi-Persistent (Stateless).Use: Conditions, sensors, and logic gates. Examples: IS_READY, HAS_FAULT, BUTTON_PRESSED.Action:Executed every single tick the tree traverses this node.It does not maintain a "completed" state.Returns true or false immediately to direct the control flow (e.g., inside an if_then or cond).3. Main Functions (!)Symbol: main("NAME") or main_ref("NAME")Lifecycle: Quasi-Persistent (Stateful).Use: Long-running tasks, state machines, and sequential operations. Examples: DELAY, MOVE_MOTOR, WAIT_FOR_ACK.Action:Executed every tick the tree reaches this node.Persistence: The function controls its own lifecycle via return codes:CFL_HALT: "I am busy/waiting. Stop the tree here and call me again next tick." (Persists).CFL_CONTINUE: "I am done. Move to the next node." (Completes).Used to block a pipeline until a physical action completes.Comparison TableTypeSymbolBehaviorPersistenceTypical ReturnOneshot@Runs once, then disables self.None (Disabled after run)void (implicitly Continue)Boolean?Re-evaluated every tick.Quasi-Persistenttrue / falseMain!Runs every tick until task done.Quasi-PersistentCFL_HALT (Busy) / CFL_CONTINUE (Done)Execution ExampleConsider a pipeline with a DELAY (Main) and a LOG (Oneshot):Luapipeline("sequence")
luajit_generators/s_expression_run_time/README.md:Tick 1:LOG_START: Runs. Sets FIRED flag.DELAY: Starts timer. Returns HALT.Result: Tree stops at DELAY.Tick 2...N:LOG_START: Skipped (Flag is set).DELAY: Checks timer. Returns HALT.Result: Tree stops at DELAY.Tick 100:LOG_START: Skipped.DELAY: Timer expired. Returns CONTINUE.LOG_DONE: Runs.Result: Tree completes.3. The DSL Generator (s_expr_dsl.lua)The DSL is a Lua-based compiler that generates optimized, read-only C data structures.Key Featuresgensym Support: Automatically generates unique names for structural nodes (e.g., pipeline()), reducing boilerplate and copy-paste errors.Static Validation: Tracks brace depth and context validity at compile time.Two-Pass Compilation: Automatically resolves forward jumps for braces and sibling links, enabling O(1) skipping at runtime.4. Comprehensive Test Suite (test_comprehensive.lua)The test program serves as both a validation suite and a reference manual for DSL patterns. Below is a breakdown of each test tree and the specific DSL capability it exercises.Basic Control FlowTreeNameDSL Concept TestedDescription1simple_pipelineSequencingVerifies basic pipeline behavior: execute children sequentially. Tests oneshot (@) firing and main (!) blocking (simulated DELAY).2if_then_testConditionalsTests the if_then composite node. Verifies that an action is only executed if the condition (boolean context) evaluates to true.3if_else_testBranchingTests if_then_else. Verifies execution of the "Else" path when the condition fails.14empty_pipelineEdge CasesEnsures the engine handles 0-child nodes gracefully without crashing.Boolean LogicTreeNameDSL Concept TestedDescription4bool_and_testShort-CircuitingTests bool_and. Verifies that evaluation stops at the first false result.5bool_or_testShort-CircuitingTests bool_or. Verifies that evaluation stops at the first true result.6bool_not_testInversionTests bool_not. Verifies boolean negation.7nested_boolComplexityTests deep nesting of boolean operators: (A AND (B OR (NOT C))).Advanced RoutingTreeNameDSL Concept TestedDescription8cond_testMulti-Way SwitchTests the cond node (Lisp style switch). It evaluates a list of clause nodes and executes the action of the first matching condition.9dispatch_testPattern MatchingTests the dispatch node. It routes execution based on a string key (e.g., event type) matching a case pattern.Functional Capabilities (Tier 2)TreeNameDSL Concept TestedDescription16data_listData StructuresTests open_brace() / close_brace() (PARAM_OPEN). Verifies the engine can pass arrays of data to a function.17sexpr_testCode-as-DataCritical Tier 2 Test. Tests PARAM_OPEN_CALL. It passes executable logic (e.g., (ADD 1 2)) as a parameter. The engine evaluates this S-Expression recursively before passing the result to the parent function.18func_refHigher-Order FnsTests passing function references (main_ref) as arguments. This allows creating generic "Decorator" nodes like REGISTER_CALLBACKS or RETRY.Engine FeaturesTreeNameDSL Concept TestedDescription10debug_testWrappersTests the dbg node, which acts as a transparent passthrough while logging messages.11oneshot_testState FlagsVerifies that oneshot nodes fire exactly once per activation lifecycle (checked via S_EXPR_NODE_FLAG_ONESHOT_FIRED).12main_returnAsync LogicVerifies that the engine respects CFL_HALT. The tree pauses execution until the leaf function returns CFL_CONTINUE.13param_typesMarshalingVerifies 32-bit/64-bit safe handling of int, uint, float, and string types.15deep_nestStack SafetyA stress test combining pipelines, conditionals, and booleans to ensure the non-recursive engine traversal handles depth correctly.5. The Runtime Environment (s_engine)The C runtime implements the Two-Tier Memory Architecture.1. The Module (ROM)Defined in s_engine_module.h. This is the read-only definition generated by the compiler.Storage: Flash / .rodata.Lifecycle: Initialized once via s_expr_module_init.Content: Function tables, string literals, and the flattened node topology.2. The Instance (RAM)Defined in s_engine_types.h. This is the mutable state for a specific execution context.Storage: Heap / Static Pool.Lifecycle: Created via s_expr_tree_create.Content: node_states array tracking flags (ACTIVE, FIRED) and user data.Integration (from main.c)C// 1. Initialize the Shared Module (ROM)
luajit_generators/s_expression_run_time/README.md:6. Host Integration Example (main.c)The main.c file serves as both a comprehensive test harness and a reference implementation for integrating the S-Expression Engine into your firmware. It demonstrates the engine's dependency injection model and multi-instance capabilities.Key ComponentsThe harness mocks a hardware environment to validate behavior trees without actual peripherals.1. Simulation State (test_state_t)Instead of accessing physical registers, the test defines a struct to hold the state of virtual hardware.Fields: led_on, counter, is_ready, delay_remaining.Mechanism: An array g_states[MAX_INSTANCES] allows multiple tree instances to operate on separate "virtual hardware" simultaneously.2. Dependency InjectionThe engine is initialized with zero global state. The main() function injects dependencies manually:Allocator: test_malloc/test_free wrappers (can be replaced with FreeRTOS heap or static pools).Registry: Tables mapping string names (e.g., "LED_ON") to C function pointers (fn_led_on).3. Execution LifecycleThe test runner performs the complete lifecycle of an S-Expression application:Module Initialization:C// Resolves all function names in the generated header to pointers in the registry.
luajit_generators/s_expression_run_time/README.md:Handling Async Logic (e.g., DELAY):The fn_delay implementation demonstrates state persistence across ticks:Checks state->state (RAM). If 0 (inactive), initializes the target delay.Decrements the remaining time in test_state_t.Returns CFL_HALT if time remains, or CFL_CONTINUE if finished.Multi-Instance DemonstrationThe run_multi_instance_test function is the critical proof of the Two-Tier Architecture.It creates two instances (inst_a, inst_b) of the same tree definition (simple_pipeline_2).It ticks them alternately in a loop.Result: You see two independent DELAY timers counting down and two independent LED states toggling, proving that the static tree definition is successfully decoupled from execution state.7. Building and RunningCompile the DSL:Bashluajit compile.lua test_comprehensive.lua
luajit_generators/s_expression_run_time/README.md:Compile the C Engine:Bashgcc -o test_runner main.c s_engine_module.c s_engine_eval.c -I.
